1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,559
Can you show me team Git workflow?

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:13,141
What happens when three people work on the same repository at the same time,

3
00:00:13,141 --> 00:00:16,259
and the small set of habits that keeps it calm:

4
00:00:16,259 --> 00:00:22,030
short branches, pull before you start, push often, integrate through the shared server.

5
00:00:23,767 --> 00:00:28,166
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

6
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:32,273
Each developer works on a branch and pushes it:

7
00:00:32,273 --> 00:00:36,833
The branches are integrated into main one by one (on GitHub:

8
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:38,962
three merged pull requests).

9
00:00:38,962 --> 00:00:43,295
Here, Ada merges all three, as the PR merge button would:

10
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:48,364
The branches are integrated into main one by one (on GitHub:

11
00:00:48,364 --> 00:00:50,479
three merged pull requests).

12
00:00:50,479 --> 00:00:54,783
Here, Ada merges all three, as the PR merge button would:

13
00:00:54,783 --> 00:00:59,163
Everyone updates their main before starting the next task:

14
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:03,694
Everyone updates their main before starting the next task:

15
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:07,116
Let me try something.

16
00:01:07,116 --> 00:01:12,163
Two developers commit directly on main and push, minutes apart:

17
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:14,664
Linus's push is rejected:

18
00:01:14,664 --> 00:01:17,806
Grace's commit reached the server first.

19
00:01:17,806 --> 00:01:23,148
Both changed the end of menu.txt, so integrating will also conflict.

20
00:01:23,148 --> 00:01:28,254
On a team, working directly on main turns every push into a race.

21
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,814
Linus integrates Grace's work (rebase his commit on top), resolves, and pushes:

22
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:39,280
Got it.

23
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:42,031
What do I need to remember?

24
00:01:42,567 --> 00:01:45,033
Many developers, one server:

25
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:48,381
integrate through it, in small pieces.

26
00:01:48,933 --> 00:01:55,258
Pull before starting, branch per task, push early, merge via PRs.

27
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:00,504
Direct commits to a shared main cause rejected pushes and conflicts.

28
00:02:02,267 --> 00:02:06,032
I keep hearing about feature branch workflow.

29
00:02:06,032 --> 00:02:07,621
What is it, really?

30
00:02:08,167 --> 00:02:10,655
The feature branch workflow in detail:

31
00:02:10,655 --> 00:02:17,006
naming, keeping the branch up to date with main while it lives, and why long-lived branches hurt.

32
00:02:18,767 --> 00:02:23,166
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

33
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:26,864
Ada starts a feature from the latest main:

34
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,539
Meanwhile Grace's change lands on main:

35
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:36,024
Ada updates her branch with main, tests, and continues:

36
00:02:37,767 --> 00:02:39,331
Let me try something.

37
00:02:39,331 --> 00:02:40,821
A long-lived branch:

38
00:02:40,821 --> 00:02:46,781
Grace starts feature/new-prices, works on it for "three weeks" without updating,

39
00:02:46,781 --> 00:02:49,761
while main keeps changing the same file:

40
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,527
Three weeks of main against three weeks of branch:

41
00:02:53,527 --> 00:02:57,012
the longer they diverge, the more overlapping changes,

42
00:02:57,012 --> 00:03:02,174
the harder the conflict, and the less anyone remembers why each change was made.

43
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:09,471
Resolve once (keep the new prices for both items), then keep the branch fresh:

44
00:03:09,471 --> 00:03:14,938
merge main in every day, or after every merged PR that touches the same area.

45
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:17,413
Got it.

46
00:03:17,413 --> 00:03:20,164
What do I need to remember?

47
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:25,069
One short-lived branch per task, named by convention.

48
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:30,223
Update the branch from main regularly; conflicts stay small.

49
00:03:30,767 --> 00:03:36,971
Long-lived branches = big conflicts; prefer small PRs and feature flags.

50
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:41,133
How does GitHub Flow work?

51
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:44,931
GitHub Flow is the simplest common team workflow:

52
00:03:44,931 --> 00:03:52,592
main is always deployable; every change goes through a short branch and a pull request; what is merged is deployed.

53
00:03:52,592 --> 00:03:55,656
We run it end to end with a tiny "deployment".

54
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:01,799
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

55
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:05,475
One change, the GitHub Flow way.

56
00:04:05,475 --> 00:04:07,243
Branch and commit:

57
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:09,693
Review and checks (here:

58
00:04:09,693 --> 00:04:16,949
the smoke test against the branch), then merge into main (the PR's merge button) and deploy:

59
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:20,319
Let me try something.

60
00:04:20,319 --> 00:04:21,399
Skip the flow:

61
00:04:21,399 --> 00:04:28,184
a "quick fix" pushed straight to main, without review or checks, breaks the prices file.

62
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:35,713
Production's smoke test failed (deploy.sh stopped with exit status 1 and printed no deployed line):

63
00:04:35,713 --> 00:04:40,859
main is no longer deployable, and every later merge is blocked behind it.

64
00:04:40,859 --> 00:04:42,058
Find the culprit:

65
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,037
The fastest safe fix on a shared main:

66
00:04:47,037 --> 00:04:52,829
revert it, through the same flow (branch, check, merge), and deploy:

67
00:04:54,567 --> 00:04:55,280
Got it.

68
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:58,031
What do I need to remember?

69
00:04:58,567 --> 00:04:59,589
GitHub Flow:

70
00:04:59,589 --> 00:05:05,466
main always deployable; branch → PR → review/checks → merge → deploy.

71
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,039
Bypassing review and checks breaks production for everyone.

72
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:13,560
Fix forward through the same flow:

73
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:14,604
a revert PR.

74
00:05:16,367 --> 00:05:18,486
Why would I need Git Flow?

75
00:05:19,033 --> 00:05:24,420
Git Flow is a branching model with two long-lived branches (main for releases,

76
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:30,082
develop for integration) and short-lived feature/, release/ and hotfix/* branches.

77
00:05:30,082 --> 00:05:37,678
We run a full cycle with plain Git commands and discuss where it helps and where it is unnecessary complexity.

78
00:05:39,433 --> 00:05:43,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

79
00:05:44,367 --> 00:05:45,169
Feature:

80
00:05:45,169 --> 00:05:48,376
from develop, back into develop:

81
00:05:48,933 --> 00:05:49,640
Release:

82
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:57,588
from develop; only stabilisation commits; merged into main (tagged) and back into develop:

83
00:05:59,333 --> 00:06:01,683
Let me try something.

84
00:06:01,683 --> 00:06:04,592
A production bug in 1.1.0:

85
00:06:04,592 --> 00:06:06,383
the latte price.

86
00:06:06,933 --> 00:06:09,569
develop still has latte 3.20:

87
00:06:09,569 --> 00:06:15,750
the next release (1.2.0, cut from develop) would bring the bug back.

88
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:19,568
Check what main has that develop does not:

89
00:06:21,333 --> 00:06:23,133
Here is the fix.

90
00:06:24,867 --> 00:06:25,580
Got it.

91
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:28,331
What do I need to remember?

92
00:06:28,867 --> 00:06:29,851
Git Flow:

93
00:06:29,851 --> 00:06:38,166
main (releases) + develop (integration) + feature, release, hotfix branches.

94
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:44,284
Hotfixes go into main and develop; check with git log develop..main.

95
00:06:44,833 --> 00:06:50,748
Good for versioned products; often unnecessary for continuously deployed services.

96
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,459
What should I know about branch protection?

97
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,654
How a server enforces the team rules:

98
00:06:58,654 --> 00:07:03,030
no direct pushes to main, changes only through pull requests,

99
00:07:03,030 --> 00:07:07,191
required reviews, required status checks, no force pushes.

100
00:07:07,191 --> 00:07:11,710
First we build the mechanism ourselves with a server-side hook,

101
00:07:11,710 --> 00:07:16,444
then we use GitHub's branch protection on the practice repository.

102
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:22,599
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

103
00:07:23,133 --> 00:07:24,274
How it works:

104
00:07:24,274 --> 00:07:32,348
the server runs a pre-receive hook before accepting a push; if it fails, nothing is updated.

105
00:07:32,348 --> 00:07:38,403
GitHub's protection is the same idea, configured instead of scripted.

106
00:07:38,967 --> 00:07:41,283
Ada pushes to main directly:

107
00:07:41,283 --> 00:07:47,486
Branches are allowed; the "merge" is done by the PR system (PUSHER=pr-bot):

108
00:07:48,033 --> 00:07:54,473
Branches are allowed; the "merge" is done by the PR system (PUSHER=pr-bot):

109
00:07:56,233 --> 00:07:57,838
Let me try something.

110
00:07:57,838 --> 00:08:03,798
Push directly to the protected main on GitHub, even as the repository's owner:

111
00:08:04,333 --> 00:08:04,797
GH006:

112
00:08:04,797 --> 00:08:12,455
Protected branch update failed for refs/heads/main and Changes must be made through a pull request:

113
00:08:12,455 --> 00:08:17,251
the rule works, including for the owner (enforced for admins).

114
00:08:17,251 --> 00:08:21,273
Nothing changed on GitHub; the commit is only local.

115
00:08:23,033 --> 00:08:26,283
The commit belongs on a branch with a PR:

116
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:28,747
Got it.

117
00:08:28,747 --> 00:08:31,498
What do I need to remember?

118
00:08:32,033 --> 00:08:38,318
Protection is enforced by the server at push time (GitHub rules ≈ a managed pre-receive hook).

119
00:08:38,867 --> 00:08:45,716
Require PRs, approvals and checks; disallow force pushes; enforce for admins.

120
00:08:46,267 --> 00:08:49,518
A rejected push changes nothing on the server:

121
00:08:49,518 --> 00:08:52,486
move the commit to a branch and open a PR.
